*** MISSING >> Helpful NullPointerExceptions (JEP 358, Java 14+, default ON since Java 15)
    >> relevant to the "run time exception" example above (NullPointerException)
    >> pre-14 message: just "java.lang.NullPointerException", no detail
    >> Java 17 message: pinpoints exactly which variable/field/call chain was null
       Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException:
           Cannot invoke "String.length()" because "s" is null
             at org.example.exception_handling.ExceptionVsError.main(ExceptionVsError.java:20)
    >> huge help debugging chained calls like a.b.c.d() - tells you WHICH link was null


*** MISSING >> try-with-resources (Java 7+, enhanced Java 9+) - directly relevant to checked exception example above
    Before (your example style):
       PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter("hello.txt");
       // must catch/declare FileNotFoundException - no auto close, risk of resource leak
    Better (Java 7+, still current in 17):
       try(PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter("hello.txt")){
           pw.println("data");
       }
       // still must catch/declare FileNotFoundException (checked), BUT pw auto-closed even if exception occurs
    >> checked-exception requirement doesn't go away - try-with-resources only guarantees
       the resource (pw) is closed automatically, doesn't remove the compile-time checking rule


*** MISSING >> Pattern Matching instanceof (Java 16+, standard in 17) - relevant to unchecked exceptions like ClassCastException
    Object obj = "hello";
    if(obj instanceof Integer i){        // safe check, no cast, no ClassCastException risk
        System.out.println(i);
    }
    >> reduces scenarios where careless manual casting causes unchecked ClassCastException at runtime


📝 QUICK NOTE - CHECKED vs UNCHECKED, JAVA 17 RELEVANT ADDITIONS

| Type              | Compiler enforced? | Java 17-relevant addition                                   |
|-------------------|---------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| Checked exception   | Yes - must catch/declare | try-with-resources still required alongside catch/declare  |
| Unchecked (Runtime) | No                  | Helpful NPE messages (Java 14+/15 default) give exact cause  |
| Unchecked (Error)   | No                  | unaffected - still lack-of-resources related, unchanged        |


*** DEPRECATED >> related to this topic

    1. new String("Hello") / new Integer(10) style object creation inside try blocks - not formally
       an exception-handling deprecation, but relevant if these appear in checked/unchecked exception
       examples: wrapper constructors deprecated since Java 9 (forRemoval later)
       >> use Integer.valueOf(10) / autoboxing / string literals instead, doesn't change checked/unchecked
          behavior, just cleaner object creation while demonstrating exception scenarios

    2. Thread.stop(Throwable) - DEPRECATED FOR REMOVAL, relevant if used to forcibly inject a
       Throwable causing unpredictable unchecked termination
       >> use cooperative cancellation (volatile flag / Thread.interrupt()) instead
